  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 359 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper low remains centered just to the east heading \ninto Monday, keeping the region in a seasonably cool airmass. A few \nshowers will continue to move through tonight, with a bit more \nwidespread coverage over Vermont as a shortwave pivots through. \nAfter the shortwave exits, dry conditions will prevail through \nMonday. Skies will clear and it should be a great early fall \nstretch. Radiational cooling will be efficient Sunday night, with \nlows generally falling into the 40s and upper 30s. The current \nforecast low at SLK is 37, but it could easily fall a few more \ndegrees if confidence increases in light winds. Warm air advection \nwill begin for Monday and Tuesday, but it will be unsuccessful in \ncausing a significant airmass change, with the humidity staying low \nand temperatures remaining around seasonable. A few showers are \npossible in northern areas Monday night and early Tuesday morning as \na dying cold front passes into the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A broad upper high will be positioned to our south \nduring the middle of next week. Between a departing trough and \nanother one moving towards James Bay, there will be a 500mb ridge \naxis that extends well north into Quebec Province. Cool air will \nremain locked in eastern Vermont Tuesday night, but clouds and warm \nadvection will begin impinging across northern New York. So eastern \nVermont will tumble into the mid 40s, along with cool hollows of the \nAdirondacks, while most the rest of the region will stay in the 50s, \nand perhaps a few spot 60s in the St. Lawrence Valley as we open up \nWednesday. Much of Wednesday will be dry, but cloudy. Winds at 850mb \nwill be speedy at 50-55 knots, but it appears we'll have a modest \ninversion below that layer. Nevertheless, the pressure gradient from \ndeparting surface high pressure and a sub-990mb low to our north \nwill promote gusty winds (at least 25-35mph) across the region by \nWednesday afternoon. This will likely bring some 70s into the \nregion, although some raw guidance suggests temperatures may be \ntowards more the upper 60s to lower 70s range. Mid to late \nafternoon, a leading surge of warm air will overrun departing cool, \nstable air and produce some rain. Synoptic guidance is likely broad-\nbrushing rain given the strong mid-level flow that will likely \nproduce strong terrain shadowing downwind mountain areas. There's \nalso plenty of dry air that must be saturated in the lower-levels. \nAs we get into the range of mesoscale guidance, this will probably \nlook less like a wash out. The occluded front and axis of a sharp \nupper trough will progress east late Wednesday evening into \nWednesday night, and will be capable of additional showers and \nperhaps a thunderstorm or two.\n\nFollowing this, we'll spend Thursday and Friday in cyclonic flow as \nthe sub 990mb low continue east. Lingering, intermittent shower \nactivity, especially in northwest facing slopes, will be possible. \nFriday's chances are somewhat more uncertain depending on how \nquickly dry air filters into Vermont and northern New York. It will \nbe a notable surge of cool air behind this area of low pressure \nlikely preventing day time highs from reaching the 70s for much of \nthe area next Friday. Broad high pressure will establish itself \nacross the weekend, signaling that the next weekend should be very \nnice.",
